In .NET 8+, the framework already ships `System.TimeProvider` — no need to create your own abstraction.

**Inject it into your class:**

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public Report Generate()
    {
        var now = timeProvider.GetUtcNow(); // instead of DateTime.UtcNow
        // ...
    }
}
```

**In production (DI registration):**

```csharp
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

**In tests (deterministic):**

```csharp
// Using Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing (NuGet package)
var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 9, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var report = generator.Generate();

// You can also advance time:
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

**Install the test helper:**

```
dotnet add tests/MyProject.Tests package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

This gives you full control over time in tests without rolling your own interface.
